course: dictionary
lesson: 8429
title: A sabre is more than a sword
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sabre is a heavy sword with a curved blade and one sharp edge. The word can mean different things, so context tells you which meaning is right. Read the words around it and ask what would fit.
explain: «The sabre-toothed cat» uses the word sabre with teeth, so sabre-toothed must mean having long, curved teeth like a sword. Try your meaning in the sentence: a cat with sword-like teeth still makes sense, so you are probably right.
explain: «Sabre-rattling» is a clue-rich phrase. Rattling makes a noise to frighten, so sabre-rattling means threatening others with force or war to scare them, without actually fighting. Words can carry a warning as well as a meaning.
fact: A sabre is a heavy sword with a curved blade and one sharp edge.
fact: «Sabre-rattling» means threatening to use force or war to scare others, without actually fighting.
fact: In fencing, the sabre is one of three weapons, with foil and épée, and points are scored by hitting with the edge or the tip.
fact: «Smilodon, the sabre-toothed cat» had canine teeth about 18 centimetres long, and it was not a tiger but a cat from a separate branch.
fact: The film Ice Age is a cartoon, but many of its animals, such as sabre-toothed cats and woolly mammoths, really lived.
exercise: Soldiers on horseback, called cavalry, carried a heavy sword with a curved blade and one sharp edge. What is it called? | Lance | *Sabre | Shield | Dagger
exercise: «The sabre-toothed cat had two very long curved teeth.» What does sabre-toothed mean? | Able to run very fast on ice | *Having long curved teeth like a sword | Living only on a warm island | Having no teeth at all
exercise: In fencing, points are scored by hitting with the edge or the tip of a sabre. What is a sabre in this sport? | *One of three weapons used in fencing | A white suit worn by fencers | A horse that fencers ride | A kind of mask worn by fencers
test: The news says two countries are «sabre-rattling». What does it mean? | Playing a loud drum in a parade | Fighting a long battle at sea | Sharpening swords for a museum | *Threatening force or war to scare others, without fighting
test: «The film mixes things up for fun: a real sabre-tooth would have hunted a young mammoth, not walked beside it.» What does this sentence suggest about the film? | It shows a tiger in the snow | *It changes real facts for fun | It is a true documentary about mammoths | Mammoths were hunted by dodos
exam: «Smilodon, the sabre-toothed cat, had canine teeth about 18 centimetres long.» What does sabre tell you about those teeth? | Made of wood and soft | *Long and curved, like a sword blade | They had no teeth at all | Short and blunt like a stone
